The mixed $\gamma$ -butyrolactone $/$ DMSO solution $\left(n\geq10\right)$ was coated onto the substrate via a consecutive two-step spin coating process at 1000 r.p.m. and 5000 r.p.m. for 10 and 60 seconds, respectively. During the second spin step, 100 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of chlorobenzene were deposited onto the substrate. The resulting films were then annealed at 100 $\mathsf{\Omega}^{\mathsf{o}}\mathsf{C}$ for 1 hour for better crystallization.
Photovoltaic Device Fabrication. A dense compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ electron transport layer $\left(50\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ was deposited onto a F-doped $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (FTO, Pilkington, TEC 15) substrate, followed by drying at $130~\mathsf{\Omega}^{\mathrm{o}}\mathsf{C}$ for 10 minutes and calcining at 500 $\mathsf{\Omega}^{\mathsf{o}}\mathsf{C}$ for 1 hour. The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ precursor solution was prepared by dissolving $890~\mathrm{mg}$ titanium isopropoxide (TTIP) and 30 mg HCl $(37\%)$ solution in $\mathrm{~8~mL}$ ethanol; the mixture was then stirred at room temperature for overnight before use. The resulting $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film was then immersed in $\mathbf{120~mM}$ of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ aqueous solution at $70{}^{\circ\mathrm{C}}$ for 1 hour, then calcined at $500~\textcircled{\cdot}\mathrm{C}$ for another 30 minutes. Then the perovskite absorber was deposited onto the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ using the method described before. The hole-transport layer was spin-coated onto the perovskite film at 4000 r.p.m. for 30 seconds using a chlorobenzene solution that containing of $63\mathrm{\mg/ml}$ of spiro-OMeTAD and 20 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of tertbutylpyridine, as well as $70\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonimide lithium salt $\mathrm{\left({170\mg/mL}\right.}$ in acetonitrile). Top electrodes were deposited using an Angstrom Engineering deposition system in an Innovative Technology glovebox. The contacts consisted of 100 nm electronbeam deposited gold deposited at $\mathbf{0.4\mathring{A}}/\mathbf{s}$ .
Relative Humidity Control. For all characterization, the perovskite films were stored at room temperature (measured as $25{\pm}1^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ) in the controlled humidity desiccator. The relative humidity $(\mathrm{RH}=90\ \%)$ ) was achieved with an accuracy of $\pm\nobreakspace3\%$ by a water/glycerol solution with careful adjustment of the weight ratio of glycerol. The precise relative humidity was measured periodically using a calibrated hygrometer.
Photovoltaic Performance Characterization. The steady-state open-circuit voltage, $V_{O C},$ is first measured using the Keithley 2400 by fixing the current to zero and sampling the voltage at multiple time points. The steadystate short-circuit current, $J_{S C},$ is measured by setting the bias voltage to zero and sampling the current at multiple time points. Instantaneous $J{-}V$ curves are then measured with a scanning rate of 0.02 $\mathrm{~V~}\mathbf{s}^{-1}$ . The maximum power point $(V_{M P P})$ is determined from the instantaneous $J–V$ curve. The steady-state power conversion efficiency, PCE, is measured by setting the bias voltage to the estimated $V_{\/{M P P}}$ . Under the bias of $V_{\/{M P P}}.$ , the current density value is sampled during 15 seconds to obtain $J_{{M P P}}$ . The PCE is finally obtained as the product of $V_{\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{M P P}}}$ and $J_{M P P}$ . The active area is determined by the aperture before the solar cell to avoid overestimating the photocurrent. Through this aperture (area $0.049\ \mathrm{cm}^{2},$ ) illumination intensity was calibrated using a Melles–Griot broadband power meter and set to be 1 sun (100 $\mathrm{mW~cm}^{-2^{\cdot}}$ ). The AM 1.5 solar power is supplied by a class A $(<25\%$ spectral mismatch) solar simulator (Science Tech). The spectral mismatch of the system was characterized using a calibrated reference solar cell (Newport). The accuracy of the power measurement was estimated to be $\pm5\%$ .
UPS Measurements. UPS measurements were carried out in an ultrahigh vacuum chamber (UHV) with a base pressure below 5x10-9 mbar. The photon line width is $\sim250$ meV and the minimum spot size $\mathbf{\alpha}\sim\mathbf{m}\mathbf{m}$ . He I photons $\ensuremath{\left(21.2\mathrm{eV}\right)}$ were used to acquire the spectra at normal emission. The photoelectrons were collected by the SPHERA U7 hemispherical energy analyzer with a 7 channel MCD detector, in Constant Analyzer Energy (CAE) mode. The binding energy values shown with 10 meV precision should be rounded to the nearest 100 meV value in accordance with the overall energy resolution.
XPS Measurements. XPS measurements were carried out in a Thermo Scientific K-Alpha system, with a $300~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ spot size, $75\mathrm{eV}$ pass energy, and energy steps of 0.05 eV. All signals are normalized to Pb for direct comparison between different samples.
XRD Measurements. XRD measurements on oriented films were conducted on a Panalytical X'Pert Pro diffractometer with a Bragg-Brentano geometry and PIXCEL 1D detector equipped with a nickel filter. Unoriented scans were collected on powders loaded into a $\mathbf{0.1-mm}$ diameter silicon capillary on a Bruker $\mathrm{D}_{8}$ Venture diffractometer using monochromated Mo Kα1 (0.71073 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ ) radiation.
Other Characterizations. The EQE spectrum for photovoltaic device is obtained using a previously published process32. Samples are tested in $\Nu_{2}$ ambient. Photoluminescence (PL) was measured using a Horiba Fluorolog Time Correlated Single Photon Counting system with photomultiplier tube detectors. For steady-state measurements, the excitation source is a monochromatized Xe lamp. For time-resolved PL, the excitation source is a laser diode with a wavelength $\therefore=\sin\theta\cdot\sin\theta$ ; the combined (source $^+$ instrumental response function) time resolution is  ns.
